#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292892 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b02be14e-cb49-5b42-8e6d-3a07498cc78a" :question/math-academy-id "q-275781" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+z &=-1 \\\\ x-y+3z &=2 \\\\ -2x+y-z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, and the inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe solution of the system of equations is \n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, \n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}, \n$\\,z=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is \n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=0,\\,z=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292893 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1472133-fedd-5702-9754-ac7f5c7dd85b" :question/math-academy-id "q-275787" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+z &=4x+y+2 \\\\ x+y &=2z+2 \\\\ y &=x-y+z+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $x=[x,y,z]^{T}$ of the system by computing the inverse of the coefficient matrix.\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$x=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x-y+z &=2 \\\\ x+y-2z &=2 \\\\ -x+2y-z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(-1+4)+(-1-2)+(2+1) \\\\  & =-9-3+3 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295036 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "224ae113-c80c-5cf2-b0fc-e86fad377055" :question/math-academy-id "q-278837" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=5\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}-\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n\n{{field-3}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(5\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{j}+(5\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(8+3)\\mathbf{i}-(20-6)\\mathbf{j}+(5+4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =11\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-14\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+9\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295037 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7575e8a2-e97b-54b4-80f9-81b1c7ceb471" :question/math-academy-id "q-278839" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨x,2,1⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,0,1⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{20}.$ Find $x.$\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\mathbf{i}-(x-2)\\mathbf{j}+(0-4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,2-x,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2-x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4-4x+{x}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+24} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+24 & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295127 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "227e2bc0-7de3-5c22-93a6-04542a843855" :question/math-academy-id "q-280314" :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,0,-2),$ $B(3,-2,1),$ $C(-1,4,-1),$ and $D(2,1,0).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(8-1)+2(-4-1)+3(-2-4) \\\\  & =14-10-18 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-14|=14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295128 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c8b632f-0961-529e-b3d4-1d020cf7c2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-280321" :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,-3,2),$ $B(4,-1,5),$ $C(-2,0,1),$ and $D(3,-2,3).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1)-2((-3)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3((-3)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3(3+1)-2(-3+2)+3(-3-6) \\\\  & =12+2-27 \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-13|=13.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295130 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77730a53-5e90-5c0a-a69e-20df2d022292" :question/math-academy-id "q-280351" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}=3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}=4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$ Find $\\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}).$\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b})=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{i}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b})=\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}\\times (2\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}\\times (2\\mathbf{b})=\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}+2(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}+2(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j})+2(4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+8\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =11\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295133 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34251e9f-6d6e-5593-b05c-d3a2ed14c7e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-280381" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}=⟨6,0,-3⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}).$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b})=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b})=\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a})-\\mathbf{a}\\times (2\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a})-\\mathbf{a}\\times (2\\mathbf{b})=3(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a})-2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Then, we use the anticommutativity property and the fact that $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a})-2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =3(\\mathbf{0})-2(-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =2(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =2⟨6,0,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295134 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b5ecce5-4519-5318-90df-e9ee9bf806a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-280385" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times (\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a}).$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times (\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a})=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}+$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times (\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a})=\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{a})=\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}-3(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\n- Then, we use the anticommutativity property and the fact that $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}-3(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}-3(\\mathbf{0}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a} & =-(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{j}+-4\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295164 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbb0f86b-7cc9-5ea8-b795-4408792fb13b" :question/math-academy-id "q-280670" :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{x}=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨0,1,-1⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨2,0,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)-2(0\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3(0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1-4-6 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-9|=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295165 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8009ac60-6b35-55bb-b7cb-31b4072308ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-280671" :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{x}=⟨1,1,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨-3,2,0⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨4,-2,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))-1((-3)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 4)+2((-3)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2+3-4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|1|=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295166 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "783ca205-66c5-547b-848c-076696d6e4cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-280712" :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,5,0⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨4,0,6⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(30)-2(0)+3(-20) \\\\  & =30-0-60 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-30|=30.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295167 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8ebdcbf-5834-5ffb-a71e-6b022b6fe2f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-280716" :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨3,1,4⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨0,2,5⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨6,0,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 0)-(0\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 6)+4(0\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =6+30-48 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-12|=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295168 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02a24008-f4b2-51f6-bfa2-34173306dcaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-280720" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨2,0,k⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨1,4,3⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨0,2,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & k \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(4\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 2)+0+k(1\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2(20-6)+2k \\\\  & =28+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =0 \\\\ 28+2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =-28 \\\\ k & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295169 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61f3e6a0-31a0-537c-a9de-e6c7ae59826e" :question/math-academy-id "q-280722" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,3,0⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,4,2⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,k,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ k & 5\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 5-2\\cdot k)-3(0\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 2)=(20-2k)+12 \\\\  & =32-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 32-2k & =0 \\\\ -2k & =-32 \\\\ k & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295170 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee4f8503-0b7f-5200-9b9f-9ac97e166c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-280729" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,k,k⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,3,2⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨4,0,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 0)-k(0\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 4)+k(0\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =15+8k-12k \\\\  & =15-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 15-4k & =0 \\\\ 4k & =15 \\\\ k & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{15}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295171 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85dbcbc6-7e0b-5364-ba3b-2e4b14e4021a" :question/math-academy-id "q-280735" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨4,{k}^{2},2⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,5,3⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,1,0⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n**In ascending order:** $\\,$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}\n$\\,$\nand\n$\\,$\n$k=${{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & {k}^{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-{k}^{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(5\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 1)-{k}^{2}(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 2)+2(1\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4(-3)-{k}^{2}(0-6)+2(1-10) \\\\  & =-12+6{k}^{2}-18 \\\\  & =6{k}^{2}-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet the triple product equal to 0:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{k}^{2}-30 & =0 \\\\ 6{k}^{2} & =30 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =5 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if \n$k=-\\sqrt{5}$ or $k=\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295172 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e746ff9c-9f50-594b-8773-1ffdd380a3c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-280834" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=⟨2,-5,-1⟩.$ Find $6\\mathbf{b}\\times (2\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}).$\n\n$6\\mathbf{b}\\times (2\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b})=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n6\\mathbf{b}\\times (2\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b})=(6\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{a})-(6\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{a})-(6\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =(6\\cdot 2)(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})-6(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =12(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})-6(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})-6(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =12(-\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})-6(\\mathbf{0}) \\\\  & =-12(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =-12⟨2,-5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-24,60,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295173 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "35b9e288-8b36-5336-a7cf-8f490e426c0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-281023" :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,0,2),$ $B(3,4,5),$ $C(6,2,4),$ and $D(5,3,6).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(6\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3)-4(5\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 4)+3(5\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2(8-6)-4(20-8)+3(15-8) \\\\  & =4-48+21 \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-23|=23.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295174 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "143e14fb-6cbe-5d90-85cc-144627915d33" :question/math-academy-id "q-281025" :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(3,0,2),$ $B(5,1,3),$ $C(4,3,5),$ and $D(3,0,5).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(3\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)-1(1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)+1(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2(9)-1(3)+1(0) \\\\  & =18-3+0 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|15|=15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295175 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ad6a5b8-f3d8-5275-887d-fa59251ace92" :question/math-academy-id "q-281029" :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(0,1,3),$ $B(0,5,5),$ $C(3,4,5),$ and $D(2,0,2).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(1\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(1\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,3,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k})-(1\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-1\\mathbf{j}-1\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4(3\\cdot -1-2\\cdot 2)+2(3\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =28-18 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|10|=10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295176 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c74fea15-7593-5e2d-a899-51b33896c8fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-281030" :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(-1,0,1),$ $B(2,-3,4),$ $C(-2,-2,2),$ and $D(0,-1,5).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+1\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-8+1)+3(-4-1)+3(1-(-2)) \\\\  & =-21-15+9 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-27|=27.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295221 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31aedb57-6eee-5721-b9e8-c3980bbe0170" :question/math-academy-id "q-281969" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨3,4,-1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨2,-5,2⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-5))\\mathbf{i}-(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(3\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(8-5)\\mathbf{i}-(6+2)\\mathbf{j}+(-15-8)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-8\\mathbf{j}-23\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-8,-23⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295222 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21cf46ef-dcff-5226-aa0a-bbfa6e2218ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-281976" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨\\sqrt{3},2,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{3}\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\sqrt{3},-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295223 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebbf8822-5fa0-5a01-b2fc-91f9cedbb9cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-281977" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨\\frac{2}{3},0,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((0)(-3)-(1)(2))\\mathbf{i}-(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(-3)-(1)(1))\\mathbf{j}+(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(2)-(0)(1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-2)\\mathbf{i}-(-2-1)\\mathbf{j}+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,\\frac{4}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295227 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dd1f450-2fd9-570b-82aa-62d00ac130e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-282143" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-2\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}-\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-3}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-4)(6)-(1)(5))\\mathbf{i}-((3)(6)-(1)(-2))\\mathbf{j}+((3)(5)-(-4)(-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-24-5)\\mathbf{i}-(18+2)\\mathbf{j}+(15-8)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-29\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-20\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+7\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295228 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "334ad845-67a7-568d-adb2-3211cbc38bf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-282144" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-3}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1-(-5)(4))\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 1-(-5)(-3))\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2+20)\\mathbf{i}-(1-15)\\mathbf{j}+(4+6)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =22\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+14\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+10\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295229 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3af77c79-40bd-5add-839b-47eafec9a2aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-282145" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=-6\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}-\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-3}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot -1)\\mathbf{i}-(-6\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{j}+(-6\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(15+2)\\mathbf{i}-(-30-8)\\mathbf{j}+(6-12)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =17\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+38\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-6\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295230 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ca86529-b09e-5f76-bd19-11fe65ed6396" :question/math-academy-id "q-282146" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{i}-7\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=5\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-6\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-3}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-7)(-6)-(4)(3))\\mathbf{i}-((2)(-6)-(4)(5))\\mathbf{j}+((2)(3)-(-7)(5))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(42-12)\\mathbf{i}-(-12-20)\\mathbf{j}+(6+35)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =30\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+32\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+41\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295231 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fcf3e73-8775-529c-878e-bbc1947caeb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-282147" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=-4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}-\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}-\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-3}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0)(-2)-(3)(2)\\mathbf{i}-(-4)(-2)-(3)(1)\\mathbf{j}+(-4)(2)-(0)(1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-6)\\mathbf{i}-(8-3)\\mathbf{j}+(-8-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-6\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-8\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295235 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3a69df9-3422-55c4-b6e2-d44c3f81e8dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-282260" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2x,3,1⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-2x,0,4⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{280}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2x & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ -2x & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 3 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(12-0)\\mathbf{i}-(8x+2x)\\mathbf{j}+(0+6x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨12,-10x,6x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}+(-10x)^{2}+(6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144+100{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{136{x}^{2}+144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{280} \\\\ \\sqrt{136{x}^{2}+144} & =\\sqrt{280} \\\\ 136{x}^{2}+144 & =280 \\\\ 136{x}^{2} & =136 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-1,$ $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295236 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e10f888-66d4-5375-8f79-b8da98cf5664" :question/math-academy-id "q-282261" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨x,0,1⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-x,2,0⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{29}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 0 & 1 \\\\ -x & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ -x & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ -x & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-2)\\mathbf{i}-(0+x)\\mathbf{j}+(2x+0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-x,2x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-x)^{2}+(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{29} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+4} & =\\sqrt{29} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4 & =29 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295237 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "999eb6f6-f5c6-519d-9ac7-4875fbf986b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-282262" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨x,3,0⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-x,0,1⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{49}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 3 & 0 \\\\ -x & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ -x & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ -x & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-0)\\mathbf{i}-(x+0)\\mathbf{j}+(0+3x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-x,3x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-x)^{2}+(3x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+{x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ \\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9} & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+9 & =49 \\\\ 10{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-2,$ $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295238 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "59e9fba3-4db6-5c48-86ed-2af0a557b731" :question/math-academy-id "q-282269" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨x,1,1⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,0,3⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{10}.$ Find $x.$\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-0)\\mathbf{i}-(3x-1)\\mathbf{j}+(0-1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,1-3x,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(1-3x)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+(9{x}^{2}-6x+1)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-6x+11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-6x+11} & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-6x+11 & =10 \\\\ (3x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295239 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f7de1193-053f-5234-9520-07973769fe4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-282270" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨x,1,2⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,0,2⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{5}.$ Find $x.$\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\mathbf{i}-(2x-2)\\mathbf{j}+(0-1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,2-2x,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2-2x)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+(4{x}^{2}-8x+4)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-8x+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-8x+9} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x+9 & =5 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x+4 & =0 \\\\ (2x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295240 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe9d7249-168a-5810-85b9-89bad6dafc21" :question/math-academy-id "q-282276" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨x,4,5⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,0,1⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{33}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4-0)\\mathbf{i}-(x-5)\\mathbf{j}+(0-4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,5-x,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(5-x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+(25-10x+{x}^{2})+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+57}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{33} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+57} & =\\sqrt{33} \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+57 & =33 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+24 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =4,\\hspace{1em}x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=4,$ $x=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295241 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d9b76997-cb19-548e-a5c8-ac57874f667e" :question/math-academy-id "q-282278" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2x,1,4⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨3,0,2⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{157}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2x & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\mathbf{i}-(4x-12)\\mathbf{j}+(0-3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,12-4x,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(12-4x)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+(144-96x+16{x}^{2})+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-96x+157}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{157} \\\\ \\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-96x+157} & =\\sqrt{157} \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-96x+157 & =157 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-96x & =0 \\\\ 16x(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\hspace{1em}x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299656 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cce553b5-2cba-5d0f-a25a-d7275ae86d94" :question/math-academy-id "q-317900" :question/problem "Consider a system of $3$ linear equations in $3$ variables, written in matrix form as $A\\,x=b.$ Here, $A$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $x$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $b$ is the column vector containing the constants. What are the missing parts of the derivation method shown below to find the unique solution of the system?\n\n$$\nA\\,x={{field-1}}\n$$\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,b\n$$\n\n$$\n{I}_{3}\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={A}^{-1}\\,b\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $A\\,x=b$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,x & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b \\\\ {I}_{3}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299700 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5898f79-8d87-5e96-a0ad-da8516834a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-31871" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${B}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $B$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${B}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299715 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a35d343-f492-5478-a8b0-3c966904f7aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-31907" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)=0$ is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of solutions” is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement “$A$ is invertible” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299747 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0876fd99-09ab-5402-abb3-c07e19b050b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-319360" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=-5.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-17$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$12$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-3$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & -12 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 12 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299749 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "228d219f-a988-51e3-add6-8253de28fd6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-319370" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=-4.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$4$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$7$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299750 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74394102-8729-5a30-8d7e-62d8f0117047" :question/math-academy-id "q-319372" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=7.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-11$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-4$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 11 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -11 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -10 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -11 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -11 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299753 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca1a8117-1d26-5acd-beac-beb6e4c1cfa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-319395" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${A}^{-1}=\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em} {{field-2}}$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-3$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$3$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$0$</td><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)(2-3)-0(\\,-2-(-3)\\,)+(-3+3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299754 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7e72b45-0695-5b36-9476-7542514f8aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-319409" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{32}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(6-0)-0(-3-0)-1(1-0) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2-0) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299756 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9cc9cf4-3e2c-5fa2-9cc3-9a41fb5db658" :question/math-academy-id "q-319413" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{12}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(6-0)-0(-2-0)-1(1-0) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0+1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299757 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8dd1f6d6-3609-5dae-86b5-57186f677ef3" :question/math-academy-id "q-319416" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{33}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(2-(-3))+2(4-0)+0(6-0) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}^{t}={c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1+4) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299758 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6120ca6-9e08-5dd7-8756-664c0b14c937" :question/math-academy-id "q-319420" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{31}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(2+1)-0\\,(0-1)+2\\,(-0-2) \\\\  & =3-0-4 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}^{t}={c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-0 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299761 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b74699a3-68fd-5996-962f-7d078766e370" :question/math-academy-id "q-319434" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{1}{3}$ and $det(A)=1-2k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=1-2k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1\\cdot k-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-2k)}\\cdot (-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{\\,2k-1\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{\\,2k-1\\,} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3k & =2k-1 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299762 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4e42e14-a47f-5067-a3e2-ccca26a8935c" :question/math-academy-id "q-319437" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-1\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1+2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-1}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{k-1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k-1 & =-2 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299764 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7cdf079-0dbd-550b-985e-14ed1ec133fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-319442" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=-2k-3.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=-2k-3\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1+2) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2k-3)}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\,2k+3\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\,2k+3\\,} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 12 & =2k+3 \\\\ 2k & =9 \\\\ k & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299765 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24df41a5-9c85-5f3d-b3b0-277ec8e095a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-319443" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-2\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}^{t}={c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(k-2)}\\cdot (k+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{k+2}{\\,k-2\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+2}{\\,k-2\\,} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2(k+2) & =-(k-2) \\\\ 2k+4 & =-k+2 \\\\ 3k & =-2 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299766 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a2668c7-61c5-574f-ad15-7bac1011df33" :question/math-academy-id "q-31946" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $U$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[U\\,|\\,I] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $U$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $U$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[U\\,|\\,I] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${U}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{U}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299776 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b5cf762-3178-5e63-a1b0-8da0e65dccf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-319546" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $17.$\n\n${a}_{13}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\,1\\cdot (1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{31} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299777 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66b7eb82-0c6f-5154-a5bd-434c9571ba55" :question/math-academy-id "q-319549" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $3.$\n\n${a}_{21}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\,(1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299778 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "959ebdb0-727b-5193-9194-4f73c8f717da" :question/math-academy-id "q-319550" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $-22.$\n\n${a}_{33}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\,1\\cdot (2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-22}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299801 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dc09a39f-9bc3-5b8d-97b8-ec01ec4b162d" :question/math-academy-id "q-319859" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ k & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))-2\\,(0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot k)+(-1)\\,(0\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot k) \\\\  & =(2+1)-2\\,(-k)+(-1)\\,(-k) \\\\  & =3+2k+k \\\\  & =3k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-(4-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3k+3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3k+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3}{3k+3} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3k-3 & =-6 \\\\ -3k & =-6+3 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-3}{-3} \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299809 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7aba959b-00b2-5aea-a09a-80fa0c6d7e58" :question/math-academy-id "q-319937" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(1\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot k)+(0\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3)+(0\\cdot k-1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =2(k-2)+3-3 \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2k-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k-2} & =-1 \\\\ 1 & =-(k-2) \\\\ k-2 & =-1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299812 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44f3f3e2-3340-514f-8bf3-b71afd4ac18e" :question/math-academy-id "q-319944" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)((-1)(-1)-(-2)(-2))+2((-2)(-1)-(-2)k)+(-2)((-2)(-2)-(-1)k) \\\\  & =(-2)(1-4)+2(2+2k)+(-2)(4+k) \\\\  & =6+4+4k-8-2k \\\\  & =2k+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-((-2)(-2)-(-2)k) \\\\  & =-(4+2k) \\\\  & =-2k-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2k-4}{2k+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k+2}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =2 \\\\ k+2 & =2k+2 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299831 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8762936c-5e8e-5984-8bdd-fb153d2e6740" :question/math-academy-id "q-32027" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find ${B}^{-1}$ by performing row operations." :question/worked-solution "We consider the augmented matrix $[B\\,|\\,I]$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[B\\,|\\,I] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  \\\\  & =[I\\,|\\,{B}^{-1}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299833 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea9e053d-85c3-58a4-8c39-2db422348c5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-32028" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-7y &=0 \\\\ -3x+6y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 6-(-7)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299834 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12e685a6-44e2-5006-b584-d214b4233509" :question/math-academy-id "q-32029" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find ${A}^{-1}$ by performing row operations." :question/worked-solution "We consider the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,I]$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,I] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}-1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  \\\\  & =[I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299837 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b398440-0508-554f-852f-fbbc5195e967" :question/math-academy-id "q-32033" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=0 \\\\ -3x+6y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 6-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299897 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60c87e6d-fc63-5e6e-a2b2-0ff3158c1fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-320792" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y &=5 \\\\ 4x+y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$1$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$5$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299898 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ddd9d97-94ee-584a-8c5b-9c16696049ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-320797" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-6y &=9 \\\\ 3y &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$3$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$4$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$9$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-6)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299899 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f32b9d41-3197-5ac1-bd1f-a220e0c2f816" :question/math-academy-id "q-320801" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5y &=6 \\\\ 2x-y &=-7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$-5$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-2$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$6$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-7$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299900 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b57a215-da2b-515b-ab92-a12875832790" :question/math-academy-id "q-320803" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7y &=5 \\\\ 2x+3y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$3$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-2$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$5$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 7}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299901 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9eb1cced-fe1c-5a4f-a0c9-4693ede93bdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-320804" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x &=6 \\\\ -3x+4y &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$4$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$-2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$6$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-5$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299902 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "363dfad0-c284-56c9-9f20-981fae3ef198" :question/math-academy-id "q-320805" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=7 \\\\ 5x+2y &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$-4$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-5$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$7$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299903 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4abd9c73-5e51-5caf-ba50-a0a3bd5f24cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-320806" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+3y &=5 \\\\ 4x+y &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$-3$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-4$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$5$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$11$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299904 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23128eb9-b5db-5f2f-87a2-462e292d0a60" :question/math-academy-id "q-320810" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=y+5 \\\\ x-2 &=-3y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$4$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$5$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y &=5 \\\\ x+3y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299905 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "963a7f3f-2112-5c95-85ab-958b0db31a33" :question/math-academy-id "q-320811" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y &=7-3x \\\\ x+1 &=-2y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$-2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$7$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=7 \\\\ x+2y &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299909 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fd1af2b-5abb-5719-b412-392f65801ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-320836" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+8 &=0 \\\\ x &=7+3y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$7$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+0y &=-8 \\\\ x-3y &=7.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299910 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "47e2d599-891b-5344-82a3-b9ce814d6215" :question/math-academy-id "q-320842" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3y-2x &=5 \\\\ x &=-1-4y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$-3$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$-2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$5$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+3y &=5 \\\\ x+4y &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299911 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e717507-c51a-5c1c-8601-5f1355a3dc79" :question/math-academy-id "q-320847" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-1 \\\\ 4x &=6-y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$-2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-4$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$6$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-1 \\\\ 4x+y &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302051 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f41cbfea-5742-50ae-9cb7-b244cecfac9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32133" :question/problem "Suppose that the following elementary row operations are applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ in the given order:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nWhat is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302055 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1aea68ff-444c-5905-8fef-602bfd366d39" :question/math-academy-id "q-32135" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=-5 \\\\ 2x+3y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302056 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74d2a5eb-cf80-54ce-b673-bcae2dc58e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-32137" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x-y &=0 \\\\ x+4y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{29}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{29}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302057 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7f53f6e-1e13-5cf9-98cc-1a6c8cbbaf2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-32138" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y &=17 \\\\ x+4y &=-17\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302061 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "292ff203-6bcf-52ab-a6fd-e1f5caf6f6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-32141" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y &=7 \\\\ 8x+y &=7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302065 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51eb37cd-4f75-568b-a384-cdd97a21011c" :question/math-academy-id "q-321437" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=8 \\\\ 4y &=5+3x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$3$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$8$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$5$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+0y &=8 \\\\ -3x+4y &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3\\text{ } & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302066 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cc52558-5774-5127-8008-8b3f9f98aa60" :question/math-academy-id "q-321438" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7y &=-14 \\\\ 2y &=5x+9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-14$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0x+7y &=-14 \\\\ -5x+2y &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 2-(-5)\\cdot 7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302067 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "652b355c-c82f-51a3-ab10-8d285623bc25" :question/math-academy-id "q-321439" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x &=3+y \\\\ 5y &=10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$0$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$3$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$10$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-y &=3 \\\\ 0x+5y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 5-0\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302068 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90efc42e-c582-5ebf-b710-fc84d57b2391" :question/math-academy-id "q-32144" :question/problem "Suppose that the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{3}$ is applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302069 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7cadcb98-fccb-55e1-8508-c8f471d2e367" :question/math-academy-id "q-321441" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=4 \\\\ 5x-y &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$4$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-6$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302070 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea5fb524-6603-5687-8a02-9759396f0c0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32146" :question/problem "Suppose that the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ is applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302071 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d615245-8860-57bd-9182-e8271d3465f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-32148" :question/problem "Suppose that the following elementary row operations are applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ in the given order:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{3}\n$$\n\nWhat is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{3}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302108 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbdb5209-5ae6-5fea-a525-87f604e2410b" :question/math-academy-id "q-32244" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+2y &=5 \\\\ -10x-10y &=-25 \\\\ 8x+8y &=20.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y &=y,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302109 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "534e2a93-e71a-5475-894e-dfd5ca99a3a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-32245" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y &=3 \\\\ 4x+ky &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & k+2 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y &=3 \\\\ (k+2)y &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nNow if $k=-2$, then the second equation is $0=-2,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when  $k=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302143 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5f8dda9-f72e-566e-91bc-44ecdce08b8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32331" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x-y &=2 \\\\ 8x+ky &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-2 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x-y &=2 \\\\ (k-2)y &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=2,$ then the second equation is $0=9,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302193 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1b8b6ae-30af-5f18-a21d-8dba558350b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-32517" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now   \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ Since we have that ${x}_{1}=4$,  we only need to find ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-6-2{x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302288 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7278c5e-97e4-5ded-971c-857a998455ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-326312" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,4)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨1,-2,-5⟩$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨0,1,-7⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\hspace{0.167em}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y+z\\hspace{0.167em}+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-3,2,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & ={\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}\\times {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7\\end{array}} \\\\  & =19\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨19,7,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =⟨-3,2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z+39 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302310 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8f8613f-ede8-5b23-9e9a-6a90b747b545" :question/math-academy-id "q-326901" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+2z+6=0$ of the plane $\\mathrm{Π}$, find an equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0.$\n\n$(\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,$\n{{field-2}}\n, \n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨2,-1,2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+2z+6 & =0 \\\\ z & =-x+\\frac{y}{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-3.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-3)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,0,-3⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,-3⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302331 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f43456bf-f295-5e3b-a9b3-15b153a5e800" :question/math-academy-id "q-32727" :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & ={s}_{11}{C}_{11}+{s}_{12}{C}_{12}+{s}_{13}{C}_{13}+{s}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+2\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =2{C}_{12} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}2} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ 1 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\left(1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =-2((-5\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 2)-0+0) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302372 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8643e81c-8c77-5255-a120-2681f124c049" :question/math-academy-id "q-32791" :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & ={t}_{11}{C}_{11}+{t}_{12}{C}_{12}+{t}_{13}{C}_{13}+{t}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+4\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =-{C}_{11}+4{C}_{13} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}-1} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}4} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ 9 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\left(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+4\\left(9\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =-0+4\\cdot 9(6\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =36\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-432\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302373 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ce9f7cf-7221-5005-8e3f-a5d9b8f1addd" :question/math-academy-id "q-32793" :question/problem "Find the cofactor of ${d}_{15}$ in \n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, to get the minor of ${d}_{15}$, we remove the $1$st row and $5$th column: \n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{gray}2}} \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 0 & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10 & {\\color{gray}∗}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the minor is \n\n$$\n{M}_{15}=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cofactor of ${d}_{15}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{15} & =(-1)^{1+5}{M}_{15} \\\\  & ={M}_{15} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304808 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62f6f273-804e-559c-a53a-0d8f68234b8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-33027" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $Q(1,2,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf{q}$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304818 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd3baba0-1430-5e29-b123-dd1e840723b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-33035" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point  $P(1,-\\sqrt{3},4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=8\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{27}\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\mathbf{i}-\\sqrt{3}\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{n}=8\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{27}\\mathbf{j}$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot (8\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{27}\\mathbf{j}) & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\sqrt{3}\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (8\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{27}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot (8\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{27}\\mathbf{j}) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304823 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00e129f5-c1b2-5333-8f70-babdfeae2e75" :question/math-academy-id "q-330498" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-2,4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,-2,4⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =⟨1,-2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z+26 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304824 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03f5292d-2cbb-5362-966c-d91bc52e2d79" :question/math-academy-id "q-330500" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,1,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,1,-5⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =⟨2,1,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =10 \\\\ 6x-2y & =10 \\\\ 6x-2y-10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
